== Discussion

The Fay-Herriot model is a versatile model in the specific contexts where it can be applied. As shown in this report, with only the information present in the ACS it is capable of improving the MSE of the original direct estimates. The Bayesian implementation of the model provides further advantages, quantifying the uncertainty of each area estimate and, by extension, improvements to estimate precision. This kind of information is not carried through with the frequentist approach, giving it a meaningful niche in comparison to it. Incorporation of spatial autocorrelation through the ICAR component showed further improvements still to estimate precision, having the narrowest intervals out of the competing models for both the coefficients and county estimates. Though the spatial covariate shown in this report lacked a meaningful effect on the estimates, it demonstrates the relative ease with which auxillary covariates can be added to the model and shows the potential for improvements that were not realized in this project. As for the best model, it depends on the priorities of the statistician. There is substantial merit to the frequentist approach with its ease of use and small size. The bayesian approaches are more computationally expensive, but are worth it if there is desire for uncertainty quantification for area estimates. 


